FEX's internal host threads (the DiskCache writer, the gdb server) showed in
the guest's own /proc: /proc/<pid>/task entries and link count, Threads: in
status and num_threads in stat. Chromium's zygote checks it is single-threaded
before forking and stops on int3, so no Chromium/Electron child started with
the disk cache on. They are now hidden from every view of the process itself.

A forked child also kept the Writer object without its thread, and shared the
parent's flock()ed cache files. The disk cache locks are taken across fork; the
child restarts its writer and reopens its own cache DB on first use.

